From cf4cd294b9b5b30c1cf85afa8db6b1dd3c83cdfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 15:33:53 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] 360 viewer: decoder writes straight into GPU memory, plus
 VR180/flat/stereo support

Upload path
-----------
The render thread used to memcpy every decoded frame from decoder-owned memory into a
single Vulkan staging buffer: 8.9 ms per 8K frame, more than half a 60Hz budget, on the
thread that can least afford it. Now the graphics backend allocates a ring of mapped
staging buffers and hands them to Video360 as its frame ring, so a decoded frame is
already where the GPU wants it. Video360 gains a two-phase startup (Open -> SetFrameBuffers
-> Start) and returns slot indices instead of pointers.

Better still, NVDEC's VRAM->RAM transfer is pointed straight at the staging slot, so there
is one PCIe crossing instead of two plus a memcpy. Falls back to the old copy path if the
driver will not take the layout (10-bit sources, where sw_format is P010 rather than NV12).
HELLO_XR_VIDEO_DIRECT=0 disables it for A/B measurement.

Also: av_find_best_stream returns ffmpeg's *default* decoder, which for AV1 is libdav1d -
and libdav1d has no hwaccel, so a card with a working AV1 NVDEC block silently decoded 8K
AV1 on the CPU with nothing in the logs to explain it. Now, when hardware decode is wanted,
we look for a decoder that can actually reach CUDA.

Measured, 8K real footage:
  HEVC 8-bit 30fps:   19 fps, 14.5 ms/frame  ->  30.0 fps, 8.2 ms/frame
  AV1 VR180 60fps:    24 fps, 12.4 ms/frame  ->  ~50 fps, 6.5 ms/frame

Projection
----------
The skybox shader only ever did full-sphere equirectangular mono. It now also does 180
half-equirectangular and flat (a virtual screen), each of which can be stereo packed
side-by-side or over-under, with the eye split applied after the spherical mapping so the
mapping never has to know about it.

Detecting which is which matters because the layouts are ambiguous from the pixel
dimensions alone - a 2:1 frame is either 360 mono or VR180 side-by-side, and guessing wrong
looks plausible rather than broken. projection360.cpp resolves it from, in order: explicit
HELLO_XR_PROJECTION / HELLO_XR_STEREO overrides, the container's own sv3d/st3d boxes,
filename conventions, and finally the aspect ratio. Whatever it settles on is printed as a
banner with the resolution, per-eye resolution and frame rate before anything is drawn -
there is no way to see the mode once the headset is on.

get360.sh
---------
Two fixes worth their own note. ffprobe prints multiple -show_entries fields in its own
order, not the order asked for, so the positional read was assigning width to $CODEC and so
on; each field is now probed separately. And YouTube serves 360/VR video only to the
`android_vr` player client - everyone else silently gets a flat monoscopic rendering at
much lower resolution (3136x1764 mono vs 7680x4096 stereo on the same URL). The script now
asks for android_vr first, falling back to the cookie-capable client for age-restricted
videos. 8-bit AV1 and VP9 no longer get re-encoded, since NVDEC handles them directly.
